      "why": "Two switches and a chain gate form a control system that has to be timed as a whole. Switch A (R at R4C4) can leave on the very first move, but it reverses three blocks at once: firing it while two or three of them are still on the board always loses (checked exhaustively over every reachable position), so in every winning line it is one of the last five moves. Switch B (Y at R6C4) must fire at exactly the right moment: after B at R2C6 has left to the right (otherwise it is reversed into the spinner beside it), and before R at R5C2 can leave, because R points into a spinner until switch B reverses it. The gate at R6C1 opens only when both of its links (P at R2C1, R at R2C2) are gone, and it stands in P's way down, so P has to be turned rather than released. Every rule of thumb fails: the luckiest hits 11 dead ends.",
